The introduction's small machine (intro.img.gz, served with this web app)
==========================================================================

The file is a Linux kernel with a small root file system inside it. waarm64 builds it
(images/build.sh intro in waarm64's repository) from:

- Linux @LINUX@, GPL-2.0-only WITH Linux-syscall-note, built by waarm64 from the unmodified
  release tarball:
    https://cdn.kernel.org/pub/linux/kernel/v6.x/linux-@LINUX@.tar.xz
    SHA-256 @LINUX_SHA256@
  with the configuration allnoconfig plus images/intro/linux.fragment (in waarm64's repository,
  at the commit this web app's About pane names).

- BusyBox 1.37.0, GPL-2.0-only: Alpine Linux's binary package busybox-static 1.37.0-r31, as it
  is. Alpine built it from its aports tree at commit @BUSYBOX_COMMIT@:
    https://gitlab.alpinelinux.org/alpine/aports/-/tree/@BUSYBOX_COMMIT@/main/busybox
  That directory's APKBUILD names the upstream tarball (busybox-1.37.0.tar.bz2, with its
  checksum) and holds Alpine's patches and configuration.

- musl 1.2.6, MIT, linked statically into BusyBox and into the programs below: Alpine Linux's
  package musl-dev 1.2.6-r3, built from its aports tree at commit @MUSL_COMMIT@:
    https://gitlab.alpinelinux.org/alpine/aports/-/tree/@MUSL_COMMIT@/main/musl
  Its full copyright file: https://git.musl-libc.org/cgit/musl/tree/COPYRIGHT?h=v1.2.6

- The programs in /toys and the scripts init, tour and cycle: waarm64's own, MIT
  (images/intro/ in waarm64's repository; their C sources are also in the file, in /toys).

waarm64's repository: https://github.com/gbl08ma/waarm64


